HB.add({
  id: "01",
  groups: [
    { id: "1.4", title: "Model Types & Scaling", items: [
      { t: `Base vs Instruct / Chat Models`,
        s: `A base model only continues text; an instruct (chat) model has been trained further to follow instructions and hold conversations.`,
        h: `A base model is the result of pretraining: it predicts the next token of internet-style text and has no idea it should answer you. An instruct model is the base model plus supervised fine-tuning on thousands of instruction–response examples, usually followed by preference tuning (RLHF or DPO) so its answers are helpful, polite and safe. Instruct models also learn a chat template with system, user and assistant roles, which is how they know which text is a rule and which is a question.`,
        u: `Use instruct/chat models for RAG answering, query rewriting, routing, judging and agents — anything where the model must follow your prompt. Use base models when you plan your own full fine-tuning, for pure text completion (autocomplete, code completion), or for research. Model hubs usually offer both, e.g. "Qwen2.5-3B" (base) and "Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct". Always check which one your server is actually running, because names in tools like Ollama can hide the difference.`,
        p: `A base model asked "How many leave days do I get?" may continue with more questions, a forum-style rambling text, or an invented policy, because it never learned to answer. It also ignores system rules like "only use the sources". Instruct tuning turns raw language ability into reliable instruction following, which is what makes grounded RAG prompts work. Picking the wrong variant is a common reason a prototype gives strange answers, and it is easy to miss because the model still writes fluent text.`,
        e: `In this platform Ollama's <code>qwen2.5:3b</code> tag is the instruct model. Sent the grounded prompt, it answers "Every full-time employee receives 24 days of paid annual leave [1]." The base model, sent the same text, tends to continue the sources list or write another "Question:" line, because to it the prompt is just a document to extend. Switching to the instruct variant fixes this immediately, with no prompt change.` },
      { t: `Reasoning Models (extended thinking)`,
        s: `Models trained to work through a problem step by step — often in hidden "thinking" tokens — before giving the final answer.`,
        h: `These models are trained with reinforcement learning on tasks with checkable answers (maths, code, logic), rewarding correct final results. They learn to write long chains of reasoning: break the problem down, try approaches, check and correct themselves. At inference time they spend many extra tokens thinking (sometimes thousands) before the visible answer; some APIs let you set a thinking budget. More thinking generally means better accuracy on hard problems, but higher latency and cost.`,
        u: `Use them for multi-step problems: applying several policy rules to a user's situation, analysing contracts, planning agent steps, writing and debugging code, and evaluation judging that needs careful reasoning. In RAG they help when the answer must be <i>derived</i> from several retrieved facts rather than quoted. For simple lookups ("what are office hours?") they are slower and costlier than needed, so route only complex questions to them.`,
        p: `Standard chat models answer in one pass and often make mistakes on problems that need several steps, such as eligibility checks with conditions and exceptions, or calculations from tables. Reasoning models reduce these errors by explicitly working through the steps. The cost is time and tokens, so production systems use them selectively. They also still need grounding: careful reasoning over wrong or missing evidence still gives a wrong answer.`,
        e: `"I joined in March 2025, I'm on probation, and I want to carry over 7 days — is that allowed?" A reasoning model retrieves the leave policy, checks the probation clause, compares 7 with the 5-day carry-over limit, notes the March 31 expiry, and answers "No — during probation carry-over is not allowed, and the maximum is 5 days anyway [1][2]." A fast chat model often misses the probation exception.`,
        n: `Do not show hidden reasoning to users as if it were a source; cite the retrieved documents instead.` },
      { t: `Mixture of Experts (MoE)`,
        s: `A model with many "expert" sub-networks per layer, where a router sends each token to only a few of them.`,
        h: `In each MoE layer the single feed-forward network is replaced by N experts (e.g. 8 or 64) plus a small router network. For every token the router scores the experts and activates only the top k (e.g. 2). The token's output is the weighted sum of those experts' outputs. Total parameters are large, but each token uses only a fraction, so compute per token is like a much smaller dense model. Training needs load-balancing tricks so all experts are used.`,
        u: `MoE models power many top open and closed models (Mixtral, DeepSeek-V3, Qwen MoE variants, and reportedly several frontier models). They are attractive when you want large-model quality at lower inference compute. For self-hosted RAG, consider them when you have enough memory to hold all experts but want faster generation per token than a dense model of similar quality. Check that your serving engine supports MoE efficiently (vLLM and llama.cpp do) before choosing one.`,
        p: `Making a dense model smarter usually means making every token more expensive to process. MoE breaks that link: knowledge capacity grows with the number of experts, while cost per token stays roughly constant. This gives better quality for the same serving compute. The catch is memory — all experts must be loaded — and more complex serving, but for many deployments the trade is worth it. Routing also lets experts specialise, for example in code or particular languages.`,
        e: `Mixtral 8×7B has about 47 billion parameters in total but activates about 13 billion per token, so it generates roughly as fast as a 13B dense model while scoring closer to much larger ones. A company serving it for RAG needs GPU memory for all 47B parameters, yet gets far more throughput than a 47B dense model would give. For a CPU-only laptop, MoE models are usually still too large to hold in memory, which is why small dense models are used there. The router's choices can be logged, showing that code questions tend to use different experts than HR questions.` },
      { t: `Dense vs Sparse Models`,
        s: `Dense models use every parameter for every token; sparse models (like MoE) use only part of their parameters per token.`,
        h: `In a dense model each token passes through every layer's full attention and feed-forward weights, so compute per token grows with model size. In a sparse model, routing or masking means each token touches only some weights — e.g. 2 of 8 experts — so total size and per-token compute are decoupled. "Sparse" can also describe sparse attention patterns, but in model-size discussions it usually means MoE.`,
        u: `Use the distinction when choosing models and hardware. Dense models are simpler: easy to quantise, fine-tune and serve on one device, predictable latency. Sparse models give more quality per FLOP but need memory for all parameters and smarter serving (expert parallelism). Small RAG deployments on CPUs or single GPUs usually pick dense models; large-scale services often pick MoE. Fine-tuning tools also support dense models more maturely, which matters if you plan LoRA adaptation.`,
        p: `Comparing a "47B" sparse model with a "47B" dense model by parameter count alone is misleading: the sparse one runs much faster but needs similar memory. Teams who do not understand this either buy too little memory for an MoE model or expect a dense model to be as fast as an MoE. Knowing the difference avoids expensive infrastructure mistakes. Latency behaviour differs too: dense models have steady per-token cost, while MoE serving can vary with expert load across GPUs. Capacity planning should use both total and active parameter counts.`,
        e: `Llama 3 8B is dense: 8B parameters, all used for every token, fits on a 16 GB GPU at 8-bit. Mixtral 8×7B is sparse: ~47B parameters to store (~26 GB at 4-bit) but only ~13B used per token. For a laptop CPU deployment like this platform, a small dense model (qwen2.5:3b) is the practical choice. In interviews, a good one-line answer is: dense = all weights per token; sparse = a routed subset per token, more capacity for the same compute. Then name one example of each, such as Llama 3 8B and Mixtral 8×7B.` },
      { t: `Parameters vs Active Parameters`,
        s: `Total parameters decide how much memory you need; active parameters per token decide how much compute (and speed) each token costs.`,
        h: `For dense models the two numbers are equal. For MoE models, active parameters = shared parts (attention, embeddings) + the experts selected for each token. Memory to load the model ≈ total parameters × bytes per parameter (2 for fp16, ~0.5 for 4-bit). Generation speed is roughly limited by reading active weights from memory for every token, so fewer active parameters mean more tokens per second. The KV cache comes on top of the weights in both cases.`,
        u: `Use both numbers for capacity planning: total parameters to choose GPUs/RAM (plus KV cache), active parameters to estimate speed and cost per token. Model cards for MoE models list both (e.g. "671B total, 37B active"). When an interviewer asks why an MoE model is cheap to run but hard to host, this is the answer. API prices often track active parameters, which is one reason MoE-based APIs can be cheaper than dense ones of similar quality. For self-hosting, total parameters decide which GPUs you must buy.`,
        p: `Without separating the two, teams either underestimate memory (an MoE model will not even load) or overestimate cost (assuming a 671B model is as slow as a dense 671B). Clear sizing prevents failed deployments and wrong cost forecasts, especially when comparing API pricing with self-hosting. It also explains benchmark claims like 'as good as a 70B model but runs like a 13B one': that is a statement about active parameters. Memory, not speed, is then the constraint. Always read both numbers before comparing models.`,
        e: `DeepSeek-V3 has about 671 billion total parameters but activates about 37 billion per token. Hosting it needs memory for all 671B weights (hundreds of GB even quantised), yet each token is computed roughly like a 37B model. A dense 3B model like qwen2.5:3b at 4-bit needs about 2 GB and computes all 3B parameters per token. So the two numbers answer different questions: 'can I load it?' (total) and 'how fast is it?' (active). A model card that lists only one of them is incomplete for deployment planning.` },
      { t: `Scaling Laws (Chinchilla)`,
        s: `Measured rules for how model quality improves as you add parameters, training data and compute.`,
        h: `Research (Kaplan et al. 2020, Hoffmann et al. 2022) found that training loss falls smoothly as a power law with model size and training tokens. The Chinchilla paper showed that, for a fixed training budget, many models were too big and under-trained; compute-optimal training uses roughly 20 tokens per parameter. Today, models meant to be cheap at inference are deliberately trained far longer than that ("over-trained") so a small model becomes as strong as possible.`,
        u: `Scaling laws explain why small modern models (1–8B) are so much better than older ones of the same size, and why labs train on trillions of tokens. They help you reason about choices like "a smaller model trained longer may beat a bigger one" and appear often in interviews about LLM fundamentals. They also explain why data quality and quantity matter as much as size. They also inform build-vs-buy decisions: pretraining your own model is rarely worth it compared with fine-tuning an existing, heavily trained one.`,
        p: `Before scaling laws, model sizes and training lengths were chosen by guesswork, wasting huge compute budgets. Clear rules let labs plan training runs and predict quality before spending millions. For practitioners, they explain why you can often run a small, heavily trained model on modest hardware and still get good RAG answers. They also show diminishing returns: each doubling of size or data gives a smaller gain, so beyond some point better data, better training methods or retrieval improve results more cheaply than raw scale. That is a strong argument for RAG plus a modest model over an ever-larger model alone.`,
        e: `By the Chinchilla rule a 7B model needs about 140 billion training tokens for compute-optimal training, but Llama 3 8B was trained on about 15 trillion tokens — over 100× more. That extra training is why an 8B model today can follow grounded prompts well enough for many RAG products. For practitioners the takeaway is simple: prefer recent small models trained on many tokens over older, larger ones. Newer generations of small models usually beat older models several times their size.` },
      { t: `Small Language Models (SLMs)`,
        s: `Language models with roughly under 10 billion parameters that run cheaply — even on CPUs, laptops or phones.`,
        h: `SLMs are trained on carefully filtered and often distilled data (outputs of larger models) to pack as much ability as possible into few parameters. Quantised to 4 bits, a 3B model needs about 2 GB of memory. They are fast and private, but weaker at complex reasoning, long instructions and broad world knowledge. In RAG, retrieval supplies the facts, so the model mainly needs to read, follow the format and cite — tasks SLMs can do well with good prompts.`,
        u: `Use SLMs for local or offline assistants, edge devices, high-volume cheap tasks (routing, classification, query rewriting, summarisation), and privacy-sensitive deployments where data must not leave the machine. Pair them with strong retrieval, short focused contexts and few-shot examples. Route harder questions to a larger model if available. They also make good 'workers' inside bigger systems: a small model rewrites queries or classifies intents while a larger model writes final answers.`,
        p: `Large models need expensive GPUs or external APIs, which may be too costly, too slow over the network, or not allowed for sensitive data. SLMs make AI usable on existing hardware and inside strict privacy boundaries. Their limits are real, so systems must compensate with better retrieval, clearer prompts and validation. They also reduce latency for interactive use, because fewer parameters mean faster tokens. And they make fine-tuning affordable: a LoRA run on a 3B model fits on one consumer GPU. These benefits make SLMs the default for many enterprise RAG deployments with strict budgets or privacy rules.`,
        e: `This platform runs qwen2.5:3b (Q4_K_M, about 2 GB) on a CPU-only laptop. With a grounded prompt plus one worked example it writes structured, cited answers; without the example it once invented "over a year of experience". The lesson: small models work well when the prompt is concrete and the evidence is clean. Routing simple questions to the small model and hard ones to a bigger model is a common way to keep both cost and quality under control. Validating citations in code also covers for the small model's occasional mistakes.` },
      { t: `Multimodal LLMs (Vision-Language)`,
        s: `LLMs that accept images (and sometimes audio or video) together with text, and reason over both.`,
        h: `A vision encoder (often a ViT) turns an image into a set of embedding vectors; a projection layer maps them into the LLM's token space; the LLM then treats them like extra tokens in the prompt. Trained on image–text pairs and visual instructions, the model can describe images, read text in them, answer questions about charts and compare documents. Image tokens count against the context window, so high-resolution pages cost many tokens.`,
        u: `In RAG they parse difficult pages (infographics, forms, slides), read charts and tables that text parsers break, caption images for indexing, and answer questions directly over retrieved page images (multimodal RAG with ColPali-style retrieval). They are also used to check screenshots in support workflows. Video-capable models extend the same idea to recorded meetings and training videos. Expect higher cost per page than text parsing, so use them selectively.`,
        p: `Much enterprise knowledge sits in diagrams, scanned forms and slides that text-only pipelines cannot read. Without vision models, these documents are either skipped or parsed badly. Multimodal LLMs make that knowledge searchable and answerable, at the cost of slower and more expensive processing and some risk of misreading. They also reduce pipeline complexity for visually rich documents: one model can read layout, text and figures together instead of chaining OCR, layout detection and table extraction. Each step removed is one less place for errors.`,
        e: `A field engineer asks "Which wire goes to terminal L1 on the ZX-200?" Retrieval returns the manual page with the wiring diagram as an image; a vision-language model reads the diagram and answers "The red wire goes to L1 (page 14, Figure 3)." A text-only pipeline would only have seen the caption "Figure 3: Wiring" and could not answer. The image page costs many more tokens than the caption, so the system sends only the retrieved page, not the whole manual. Spot-checking a sample of VLM transcriptions catches occasional misreadings.` },
      { t: `Open-weight vs Closed (API) Models`,
        s: `Open-weight models can be downloaded and run on your own hardware; closed models are only available through a provider's API.`,
        h: `Open-weight models (Llama, Qwen, Mistral, Gemma, DeepSeek) publish their weights under a licence; you host them with Ollama, vLLM or TGI, can fine-tune them, and control where data goes. Closed models (GPT, Claude, Gemini) run on the provider's servers; you send requests and pay per token. Licences differ: some open models restrict commercial use or user counts, so read them. Hosted open models (through cloud providers) sit in between: open weights, but someone else runs them.`,
        u: `Choose open-weight models when data must stay inside your network (regulated industries, data residency), when you need offline operation, fine-tuning freedom, or predictable cost at high volume. Choose closed APIs for top capability, fast start without infrastructure, and variable workloads. Many teams mix them: open models for embeddings, reranking and routine answers, an API model for hard reasoning.`,
        p: `Sending confidential documents to a third-party API may break contracts or regulations; running your own model needs hardware and ops skills. Understanding the trade-off lets you meet privacy and cost requirements without giving up quality where it matters. It also protects you from sudden model deprecations or pricing changes on the API side. Vendor lock-in is another factor: prompts and evaluation tuned for one API can need rework when switching. A gateway layer such as LiteLLM keeps the application independent, so you can move between open and closed models as needs change.`,
        e: `This platform is fully open-weight and local: e5 embeddings, a MiniLM reranker and qwen2.5:3b in Ollama, so no HR document text ever leaves the laptop. A bank with stricter needs runs a larger open model on its own GPUs, while a marketing team uses a closed API for public content. Through LiteLLM, switching the platform between a local model and an API model is a configuration change. The evaluation gates then decide whether the switch is worth it, using the same golden set.` },
      { t: `Emergent Abilities`,
        s: `Abilities that seem to appear suddenly once models become large enough, such as multi-step arithmetic or following complex instructions.`,
        h: `Early studies plotted task accuracy against model size and saw near-zero performance followed by a sharp jump at some scale. Later research argued that much of the "suddenness" comes from all-or-nothing metrics (exact match); with smoother metrics, ability grows more gradually. Either way, larger and better-trained models can do tasks smaller ones cannot, and data quality and training methods (instruction tuning, reasoning RL) unlock abilities too.`,
        u: `Use the idea to set realistic expectations: small models handle simple grounded lookups but may fail at multi-rule reasoning, long instruction lists or careful synthesis. Test the abilities your use case needs — citation discipline, abstention, table reading — on the actual model, rather than assuming capabilities from a larger cousin. Keep a short list of 'must-have behaviours' in your golden set (cite sources, abstain, follow format) and run it on every model you consider. That list turns vague capability claims into a pass/fail check.`,
        p: `Teams prototype with a large API model, then switch to a small local model to save money and discover that some behaviours disappear. Knowing that abilities depend on scale and training helps plan evaluations and choose model sizes deliberately instead of being surprised in production. The debate itself is a good reminder to measure abilities with metrics that capture partial progress. In product terms, the question is not whether an ability 'emerges' but whether this model, at this size and price, reliably does what your users need. Only your own evaluation answers that.`,
        e: `A team's prototype with a large model followed seven formatting and grounding rules perfectly. The 3B local model followed only three reliably. Rewriting the prompt into fewer rules plus one example, and validating citations in code, recovered most of the quality — an engineering answer to a scale limit. The rest — complex synthesis across many documents — was routed to a larger model only when needed. That combination kept most traffic on the cheap local model.` },
    ]},
    { id: "1.5", title: "Decoding & Generation", items: [
      { t: `Next-token Prediction`,
        s: `An LLM writes text by repeatedly predicting the most suitable next token and appending it.`,
        h: `Given all tokens so far, the model outputs a probability for every token in its vocabulary. A decoding strategy chooses one (the most likely, or a sample). The chosen token is appended and the process repeats until an end token, a stop sequence or the maximum length. Training teaches exactly this skill: for every position in trillions of tokens of text, raise the probability of the token that actually came next (cross-entropy loss).`,
        u: `This is the mechanism behind every generation setting you configure — temperature, top-p, max tokens, stop sequences — and behind streaming (tokens are sent as they are produced). It also explains RAG prompt design: the model continues the text you give it, so a clear structure ("Sources: … Question: … Answer:") makes the desired continuation the most likely one. It also explains why ending the prompt with 'Answer:' or with the assistant marker matters: it makes an answer the natural next thing to write. Few-shot examples work for the same reason.`,
        p: `It explains the core risk of LLMs: they produce plausible continuations, not verified facts. If the prompt lacks the needed information, the most likely next tokens may still form a confident-sounding answer — a hallucination. Grounding, abstention instructions and citation checks exist because next-token prediction alone has no notion of truth. It also explains why models are bad at some tasks, like exact counting or long arithmetic: they predict plausible digits rather than calculating. Systems therefore hand such work to tools (calculators, SQL) and give the model the result to report.`,
        e: `For "The capital of France is", the token "Paris" gets by far the highest probability. For "Every full-time employee receives" followed by sources saying 24 days, the model gives "24" a high probability because attention sees it in the sources. Remove the sources and the model may predict "20" or "25" — whatever is common in its training data. This is why the grounded prompt both supplies sources and instructs 'If they do not contain the answer, reply NO_ANSWER' — it makes abstaining a likely, acceptable continuation.` },
      { t: `Greedy Decoding`,
        s: `Always pick the single most likely next token.`,
        h: `At each step take the token with the highest probability (argmax). It is deterministic: the same prompt gives the same output (on the same hardware and software). It is fast and simple, but it can get stuck in repetitive loops and can miss a better overall sentence that starts with a slightly less likely token. Temperature 0 in most APIs means greedy decoding. Even greedy outputs can vary slightly across GPUs or batch sizes because of floating-point differences, so do not rely on bit-exact reproducibility.`,
        u: `Use greedy decoding for tasks with one right answer: extraction, classification, routing decisions, JSON generation, query rewriting, and validation or repair steps. Many teams also use it (or near-zero temperature) for factual RAG answers to make outputs reproducible and easier to evaluate. For long creative or conversational answers, a little sampling (temperature 0.3–0.7) often reads more naturally. Choose per task, not one setting for the whole system.`,
        p: `Randomness is unwanted when you need consistent, testable outputs — the same question should give the same extracted fields or the same route. Greedy decoding removes sampling variance, which makes regression testing and debugging much easier. Its weakness is occasional repetition in long text, handled with repetition penalties or low-temperature sampling. It also makes evaluation fairer: when comparing two prompts or pipeline versions, sampling noise can hide or exaggerate real differences.`,
        e: `This platform's citation-repair attempt uses temperature 0, so if a draft cites a non-existent source, the corrected answer is deterministic and easy to compare across runs. A router that must output "hr", "it" or "finance" also runs greedy so the same question always takes the same path. Evaluation runs also use very low temperature so that differences between pipeline versions reflect the change, not random sampling. Without that, a version could pass or fail the gates by chance.` },
      { t: `Temperature Sampling`,
        s: `Sample the next token from the probability distribution, made sharper or flatter by a temperature setting.`,
        h: `Logits are divided by the temperature T before softmax. T &lt; 1 sharpens the distribution (the top token becomes even more likely); T &gt; 1 flattens it (less likely tokens get more chance). The next token is then drawn at random according to these probabilities. T close to 0 behaves like greedy decoding; T around 0.7–1.0 gives varied, creative text. Temperature changes nothing about what the model knows; it only changes how adventurous the choice among its predictions is.`,
        u: `Use low temperature (0–0.2) for grounded RAG answers, summaries of documents and structured outputs; medium (0.5–0.8) for natural conversation and paraphrasing; high (≥ 0.9) for brainstorming, marketing copy or generating diverse synthetic data. Combine with top-p for good-quality randomness. Agents and tool-callers usually run at low temperature for reliable decisions. Evaluation judges also use low temperature for consistent scores.`,
        p: `One fixed decoding style does not fit all tasks: factual answers need consistency, creative tasks need variety. Temperature is the simple knob to control this. Misconfigured temperature is a common production bug — a factual assistant left at 1.0 gives different and sometimes wrong numbers for the same question. It also affects evaluation: a sampled system needs several runs per question to measure reliably, because a single run can be lucky or unlucky. For RAG, low temperature reduces variance and makes faithfulness metrics more stable.`,
        e: `Asking "How many leave days?" five times at temperature 1.0 may produce "24", "24", "about 24", "24 or more" and one invented "26". At temperature 0.1 all five answers say "24 days [1]". For generating 300 varied synthetic test questions, the same model at 0.9 produces much more diverse phrasings. This platform uses 0.1 for answers and 0.0 for repairs, and its evaluation results are stable from run to run. Raising it would mainly add variety, not accuracy.` },
      { t: `Top-k Sampling`,
        s: `Sample only from the k most likely next tokens (e.g. the top 40).`,
        h: `Sort the vocabulary by probability, keep the top k tokens, set all others to zero, renormalise and sample. This removes the long tail of very unlikely tokens that can derail text. The weakness is that k is fixed: when the model is very sure, 40 options is too many; when it is unsure, 40 may cut off reasonable choices. Setting k = 1 is identical to greedy decoding. Typical values are 20–50; lower k makes text safer and more predictable, higher k more varied.`,
        u: `Top-k is a common default in local inference engines (Ollama, llama.cpp default top_k = 40) and is often combined with top-p and temperature. Use it for open-ended generation; for factual RAG answers, low temperature matters more than k. When outputs look random or off-topic at higher temperatures, lowering k is a quick fix. When outputs feel repetitive, raising k (with top-p) adds variety. It is mostly a tuning knob for open-ended text rather than for grounded answers.`,
        p: `Pure random sampling over the whole vocabulary occasionally picks nonsense tokens, producing broken words or off-topic jumps. Top-k cuts these off cheaply and makes sampled text more coherent. It is also cheap to compute and easy to reason about, which is why it is a default in many local runtimes. Its fixed size is the main weakness: the right number of options changes from token to token. Top-p was introduced to fix exactly that, so the two are often used together.`,
        e: `With top_k = 40, when predicting the word after "annual", the model chooses only among its 40 best candidates ("leave", "report", "budget"…) and can never pick an unrelated rare token from the 150,000-token vocabulary, even at higher temperature. If an Ollama model occasionally produces a strange word in creative mode, try top_k 20; for grounded RAG, temperature 0.1 already makes k almost irrelevant.` },
      { t: `Top-p (Nucleus) Sampling`,
        s: `Sample from the smallest set of tokens whose probabilities add up to p (e.g. 0.9).`,
        h: `Sort tokens by probability and add them until the cumulative probability reaches p; sample only from that "nucleus". When the model is confident, the nucleus may be one or two tokens; when it is unsure, it grows to many. This adapts automatically, which is why top-p is the default sampling method in most chat APIs, usually together with temperature. Common defaults are top_p = 0.9–0.95 with temperature 0.7 for chat.`,
        u: `Use top-p around 0.9–1.0 with a moderate temperature for natural chat and writing. For grounded RAG answers, keep temperature low; top-p then has little effect. When tuning generation, change temperature or top-p, not both aggressively at once, so you understand the effect. For agents and JSON outputs, keep temperature near 0 so the nucleus is effectively one token. For synthetic data generation, a higher top-p (0.95) plus temperature 0.9 gives diversity.`,
        p: `Fixed top-k is too wide when the model is sure and too narrow when it is not. Top-p keeps randomness proportional to genuine uncertainty, giving fluent, varied text without nonsense. It is also more robust across different contexts in one answer: confident parts (names, numbers from sources) stay precise, while open-ended parts (phrasing) keep some natural variety. That balance is hard to reach with temperature alone. This is why most chat products use top-p by default.`,
        e: `If the next-token probabilities are 0.60, 0.25, 0.10 and then many tiny ones, top-p 0.9 keeps exactly the first three tokens. If the probabilities are spread evenly over 30 plausible words, the nucleus grows to include most of them. In the RAG answer, after 'You can carry over' the next token '5' alone covers 97% probability, so with top-p 0.9 only '5' is possible — the number from the source is kept even with sampling on.`,
        c: `
import numpy as np
def top_p_sample(probs, p=0.9):
    order = np.argsort(-probs)
    cut = np.searchsorted(np.cumsum(probs[order]), p) + 1
    keep = order[:cut]
    q = np.zeros_like(probs); q[keep] = probs[keep]
    return np.random.choice(len(probs), p=q / q.sum())` },
      { t: `Beam Search`,
        s: `Keep the best B partial sentences at each step and finally return the highest-scoring complete one.`,
        h: `Instead of committing to one token per step, beam search keeps B candidates (beams). Each step extends every beam with its likely next tokens, scores all extensions by total log-probability, and keeps the top B. At the end it picks the best finished sequence, often with a length penalty so it does not favour very short outputs. It explores more options than greedy decoding but costs about B times more compute.`,
        u: `Beam search is common in translation, speech recognition and some summarisation models, where there is a "most correct" output. It is rarely used for chat LLMs, because it tends to produce bland, generic and repetitive text. You may still see it in seq2seq components of a pipeline (e.g. a translation step for cross-lingual RAG). It also helps constrained tasks such as generating exact titles or codes from a fixed list. Its cost grows with the beam width, so keep B small (2–5).`,
        p: `Greedy decoding can choose a good first word that leads to a poor sentence overall. Beam search finds higher-probability full sequences, which improves tasks with a single correct output. For open-ended chat, the most probable sequence is often dull, which is why sampling won there. It also gives several ranked alternatives for free, which is useful when a later step (or a person) chooses among candidates. Its tendency toward safe, short and repetitive phrasing makes it a poor fit for conversational answers, which is why chat models use sampling instead.`,
        e: `A machine-translation step translating a Tamil question into English uses beam width 4: it compares four candidate translations and returns "How many days of leave can I carry over?" rather than greedy's slightly awkward "How many leave days can be carried?". For chat answers the same team uses low-temperature sampling instead, because beam search made answers short and generic. Each method is matched to the step where it helps.` },
      { t: `Repetition / Frequency Penalty`,
        s: `Lowering the probability of tokens that have already appeared, to stop loops and repetitive text.`,
        h: `A repetition penalty divides (or multiplies) the logits of tokens already in the output by a factor such as 1.1. Frequency penalty subtracts an amount proportional to how often a token has appeared; presence penalty subtracts a fixed amount once a token has appeared at all. They push the model toward new words. Too strong a penalty forces awkward synonyms or blocks needed repeated terms like names and numbers.`,
        u: `Use mild penalties for small local models that loop, for long generations, and for list generation where duplicates are unwanted. Keep them low for RAG answers that must repeat exact terms (product names, policy names, numbers). Tune by inspecting outputs, not by default copying. Penalties interact with temperature: if you raise both, outputs can become erratic, so change one at a time. Check long answers and lists after any change.`,
        p: `Small models in particular fall into loops: "The policy says the policy says…" or repeating the same bullet. Penalties are a cheap fix at decoding time, without retraining. Repetition wastes tokens, slows answers (decode is slow), hurts readability and makes users distrust the system. A small penalty often fixes loops in small or heavily quantised models immediately. Because penalties also discourage legitimate repeats, they must be tuned with real examples. They are a decoding-time fix, not a substitute for a better prompt or model.`,
        e: `A 1.5B model summarising tickets kept repeating "Customer reported the issue" in every bullet. Setting repeat_penalty to 1.15 in Ollama removed the loop. When the same setting was applied to answers that must repeat "ZX-200" several times, it started writing "the device" instead — so the team used 1.05 there. So penalties are tuned per task, not set globally. The final settings were documented with the prompt version, so the same behaviour is reproduced after redeploys.` },
      { t: `Stop Sequences`,
        s: `Strings that make generation stop as soon as the model produces them.`,
        h: `Besides the model's end token, you can pass stop strings such as "\\nQuestion:" or "&lt;/answer&gt;". The server checks the generated text and stops when one appears, not returning it. This keeps the model from continuing past the part you want, especially in few-shot prompts where it might write a new example, or in raw-completion prompts. Servers usually exclude the stop string from the returned text, so parsing stays simple.`,
        u: `Use stop sequences for few-shot and completion-style prompts, structured formats (stop after a closing tag), tool-call formats in custom agents, and to save tokens and time. With proper chat templates the end token usually suffices, but stop sequences are a safety net. They are also a cheap safeguard against runaway generations that would burn tokens. When migrating prompts between models, re-check stop strings, because different models end their turns differently.`,
        p: `Without them, models often keep going: after answering they invent the next "Question:" and answer it too, wasting tokens and confusing users. Stop sequences end generation exactly where the format says. Uncontrolled continuation also leaks confusing content into answers: invented user turns, extra examples, or reasoning meant to be hidden. In streaming UIs, these appear on screen before you can remove them. A well-chosen stop sequence prevents that entirely and saves money on long tails of useless tokens.`,
        e: `A few-shot rewriting prompt ends each example with "\\n\\n". Setting <code>stop=["\\n\\n"]</code> returns just the rewritten query, instead of the query followed by three invented extra examples. This also cut latency, because the model stopped after about 20 tokens instead of 200. In agent loops, 'Observation:' is a classic stop string, so the model stops and lets your code run the tool instead of imagining the result.` },
      { t: `Constrained / Grammar-guided Decoding`,
        s: `Restricting each next token so the output must follow a grammar, regular expression or JSON schema.`,
        h: `At every step, the decoder computes which tokens are allowed by the grammar given the text so far and masks all others before sampling. The model can only produce outputs that match the format, e.g. valid JSON with required fields, or exactly one of a set of labels. Libraries such as Outlines, llama.cpp grammars, vLLM guided decoding and provider "structured outputs" implement this. Because invalid tokens are removed before sampling, the model's probability mass is redistributed only over valid continuations.`,
        u: `Use it whenever code consumes the output: routing labels, extracted fields, tool arguments, evaluation verdicts, query plans. It is especially valuable with small models, which often produce slightly broken JSON when only asked nicely. Ollama supports a JSON schema in its format parameter, and many APIs offer strict structured outputs. Keep schemas simple; very complex grammars can slow decoding or confuse the model's content even when the format is valid.`,
        p: `"Please answer in JSON" still produces trailing commas, missing quotes or extra commentary some of the time, and every failure needs retries or crashes the pipeline. Constrained decoding makes invalid output impossible, which simplifies code and improves reliability. It also removes a whole class of security and stability issues: code that parses model output no longer needs fragile cleanup or regex extraction. Constraints guarantee format, not correctness, so values still need validation (e.g. a route must also make sense). Combining both gives robust pipelines.`,
        e: `A router constrained to the JSON schema <code>{"route": "hr" | "it" | "finance", "needs_retrieval": boolean}</code> always returns parseable output, even from a 3B model. Before constraints, about one response in fifty needed a retry because of formatting errors. Extraction of invoice fields with a strict schema likewise eliminated all JSON parsing errors, leaving only genuine content errors to review. The remaining checks focus on whether the chosen route was right, not on parsing.` },
      { t: `Logprobs & Token Probabilities`,
        s: `The log-probability the model assigned to each generated token (and to alternatives) — a view into how sure it was.`,
        h: `Many APIs and servers can return, for each output token, its logprob and the top alternative tokens with theirs. A logprob near 0 means the model was confident (probability near 1); a very negative value means it was unsure. Averaging or taking the minimum over key tokens gives a rough confidence score; comparing label tokens gives a classifier score. Summing logprobs over an answer gives the total log-likelihood, often normalised by length for comparison.`,
        u: `Use logprobs for confidence estimation (flag answers whose key numbers were low-probability), active retrieval (FLARE retrieves when upcoming tokens are uncertain), LLM-as-classifier scores, and debugging (seeing which alternatives the model considered). Not every API exposes logprobs (or exposes them for all models), and local servers vary, so check before designing around them. Calibrate any logprob-based threshold on labelled examples.`,
        p: `LLM output looks equally confident whether the model knows the answer or is guessing. Logprobs expose the difference, giving a cheap signal for when to double-check, retrieve more or abstain. Logprobs are not perfectly calibrated — models can be confidently wrong — so treat them as one signal among several (retrieval scores, citation checks, judges). Still, they are almost free to collect and catch many shaky answers. They also help diagnose prompts: low-confidence tokens show where instructions are unclear.`,
        e: `In an answer "You can carry over 5 days", the token "5" had probability 0.97 — consistent with the source. In another answer about a policy not in the sources, the key number had probability 0.31, so the system flagged the answer for verification and it turned out to be invented. Logging these probabilities for a week showed that most later-reported errors had at least one low-probability number, which justified adding the check.` },
    ]},
    { id: "1.6", title: "Inference Optimization & Serving", items: [
      { t: `Quantization (INT8 / INT4)`,
        s: `Storing model weights (and sometimes activations or the KV cache) with fewer bits, to cut memory and speed up inference.`,
        h: `Weights are normally 16-bit floats (2 bytes each). Quantisation maps them to 8-bit or 4-bit integers with per-group scale factors, so a model needs 2–4× less memory. During inference the integers are converted back on the fly (or used directly by special kernels). Good methods keep quality loss small, usually a little worse at 4-bit than at 8-bit; very small models lose relatively more than big ones.`,
        u: `Use quantised models for local and edge deployments, CPU inference, fitting larger models on smaller GPUs, and cutting serving costs. In RAG, a 4-bit 7–8B model on one GPU often beats a 16-bit 3B model in quality at similar cost. Always evaluate the quantised model on your golden set, not only the full-precision one. KV-cache quantisation (8-bit) is a separate option that saves memory for long contexts. Start with 8-bit when quality is critical and move to 4-bit after measuring.`,
        p: `A 7B model in 16-bit needs about 14 GB just for weights, too much for many GPUs and most laptops. At 4-bit it needs about 4–5 GB. Generation speed is limited by memory bandwidth, so smaller weights also mean faster tokens. Quantisation is what makes local LLMs practical. It also lowers cost in the cloud: a model that fits on one smaller GPU instead of two larger ones can cut serving cost several times. The trade-off is a small quality drop that varies by task — reasoning and exact recall may suffer more than casual chat. That is why quantised models must pass the same evaluation as any other change.`,
        e: `qwen2.5:3b in Ollama uses the Q4_K_M quantisation: about 2 GB instead of about 6 GB in 16-bit, which is why it fits alongside the database, search engines and embedder on this 7.5 GB laptop. Its answers on the evaluation set were good enough to pass the release gates. The Q8_0 version would be slightly more accurate but needs about 3.5 GB, which would not fit comfortably next to the other services. The team measured both before choosing.` },
      { t: `GPTQ / AWQ / GGUF Formats`,
        s: `Common quantisation methods and file formats: GPTQ and AWQ for GPUs, GGUF for llama.cpp and Ollama (CPU and GPU).`,
        h: `<b>GPTQ</b> quantises a model layer by layer, using a small calibration dataset and second-order information to minimise the error introduced. <b>AWQ</b> (activation-aware weight quantisation) finds the small fraction of weights that matter most for typical activations and protects them. <b>GGUF</b> is llama.cpp's file format; it stores weights in "k-quant" types like Q4_K_M, Q5_K_M or Q8_0, mixing precisions per tensor.`,
        u: `Pick the format your runtime supports: vLLM and TGI serve GPTQ and AWQ models efficiently on GPUs; Ollama and llama.cpp use GGUF on CPUs, Apple Silicon and GPUs. Within GGUF, Q4_K_M is a common size/quality balance, Q5_K_M or Q8_0 when memory allows. Download quantised models from trusted publishers or verify checksums, since model files are part of your supply chain. Name the exact quant type in your deployment docs so results are reproducible.`,
        p: `Naive rounding to 4 bits damages model quality noticeably. These methods keep most of the quality at low bit widths, and standard formats let you download ready-made quantised models instead of quantising yourself. They also standardise distribution: one model can be published in several quantisations, so teams pick the size that fits their hardware without running quantisation themselves. Different methods suit different hardware, which is why the same model often appears in all three formats. Mixing up formats and runtimes is a common setup error.`,
        e: `A team serves "Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct-AWQ" on a single 24 GB GPU with vLLM for its RAG API, while developers run the GGUF Q4_K_M version of the same model in Ollama on their laptops for testing — the same model family, quantised two ways for two runtimes. Both builds were evaluated on the same golden set; the GPU build scored slightly higher, so laptop results are treated as a conservative baseline. Both are documented with their exact quant type.` },
      { t: `Speculative Decoding`,
        s: `A small draft model proposes several tokens ahead; the big model checks them all in one pass and keeps the correct ones.`,
        h: `The draft model quickly generates k tokens. The large target model then processes the prompt plus those k tokens in a single forward pass and checks each against its own probabilities. Accepted tokens are kept; at the first rejection, the large model's own token is used and drafting resumes. A careful acceptance rule makes the output distribution identical to the large model's. Speed-ups of about 2–3× are common when drafts are often accepted.`,
        u: `Use it to reduce latency of large models in chat and RAG answering, especially when GPU compute is available but per-token decoding is slow. Serving engines like vLLM and TensorRT-LLM support draft models, n-gram drafting (copying from the prompt), and newer variants like Medusa or EAGLE heads. Gains depend on how predictable the text is: extraction and RAG answers that copy source phrases benefit most; creative text benefits less.`,
        p: `Decoding is sequential and memory-bound: each token needs a full pass through the big model. Speculative decoding lets one big pass verify several tokens, cutting time per answer without changing quality. Large models are often limited by memory bandwidth, leaving compute idle while each token is produced. Speculative decoding uses that spare compute to verify several tokens at once. Because the output is mathematically unchanged, it is a 'free' speed-up — no quality trade-off, only extra serving complexity.`,
        e: `A RAG service answering from retrieved policy text uses n-gram (prompt-lookup) speculation: many answer phrases are copied from the sources, so drafts are accepted often and answers stream about twice as fast, with exactly the same text the large model would have produced. Users see the same answers, just faster, and evaluation scores are unchanged — which is how the team confirmed the setting was safe to enable.` },
      { t: `Continuous Batching`,
        s: `Serving many requests together and adding or removing requests from the batch at every generation step.`,
        h: `Static batching waits for a batch of requests, runs them together, and only starts new ones when the longest finishes — the GPU idles while short answers wait. Continuous (in-flight) batching schedules at the token level: when one request finishes, a new one joins immediately, and prefill of new prompts is interleaved with decoding of running ones. GPU utilisation and throughput rise dramatically.`,
        u: `It is the core of modern serving engines (vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang). Use these engines for multi-user GPU serving of your RAG answer model; Ollama is fine for single-user or small setups but is not optimised for high concurrency. Configure the maximum number of concurrent sequences and tokens per batch to balance latency and throughput. Monitor queue time separately from generation time. It matters most when many users chat at once.`,
        p: `Requests have very different lengths. With static batching, throughput collapses and latency becomes unpredictable. Continuous batching keeps the GPU busy, allowing many more users per GPU and lowering cost per answer. It also smooths latency: a short question no longer waits behind a long summary. For a RAG service with mixed workloads — quick lookups and long reports — this is essential for a good user experience. It is one of the main reasons purpose-built serving engines beat hand-written servers by large factors.`,
        e: `A company moved its RAG assistant from a simple Python server (one request at a time) to vLLM. With continuous batching, the same GPU handled about 40 concurrent conversations at similar per-user speed instead of one, cutting the number of GPUs needed for launch. Per-user latency stayed similar because short answers no longer waited for long ones to finish. Cost per answer dropped sharply. Monitoring dashboards now show queue time near zero at normal load.` },
      { t: `PagedAttention (vLLM)`,
        s: `Storing the KV cache in small fixed-size blocks, like operating-system memory pages, instead of one big contiguous chunk per request.`,
        h: `Without paging, the server must reserve KV-cache memory for each request's maximum possible length, wasting most of it (fragmentation). PagedAttention allocates blocks on demand as a sequence grows, keeps a block table per request, and lets requests share identical prefix blocks (e.g. the same system prompt). Attention kernels read the scattered blocks efficiently. This design was introduced with vLLM in 2023 and is now copied by other engines.`,
        u: `This is why vLLM fits more concurrent requests in the same GPU memory, and it is the basis of its prefix caching. Use vLLM (or engines with similar paging) when you serve many users with long prompts, such as RAG prompts full of retrieved sources. Tune gpu_memory_utilization and max_model_len so enough blocks are available for your expected concurrency. Watch for preemptions in logs, which mean the cache ran out of blocks.`,
        p: `KV-cache fragmentation used to waste a large share of GPU memory, limiting batch sizes and throughput. Paging nearly eliminated that waste, letting each GPU serve several times more requests. Memory waste meant GPUs ran far below capacity, and a few long requests could block many short ones. Paging lets the server admit as many requests as actually fit, increasing throughput and reducing cost per answer. It also enables prefix sharing, which further saves memory for common prompts.`,
        e: `Fifty users share the same 1,500-token system prompt and few-shot example. With PagedAttention and prefix sharing, the blocks for that prefix are stored once and referenced by all fifty requests, so GPU memory goes to each user's unique sources and history instead. The same GPU that previously handled about a dozen concurrent RAG requests could then handle several times more. Shared-prefix blocks for the common system prompt saved further memory.` },
      { t: `Prefix / Prompt Caching`,
        s: `Reusing the already-computed KV cache for a prompt prefix that was seen before, so it is not processed again.`,
        h: `When a new request starts with exactly the same tokens as an earlier one (system prompt, instructions, examples, or a long document), the server reuses the stored keys and values for that prefix and only processes the new tokens. vLLM and SGLang do this automatically when enabled; Ollama reuses the cache within a conversation; API providers offer cheaper "cached input tokens". Caches are matched on exact token prefixes, so even a changed space or date breaks the match.`,
        u: `Put static content first in prompts — system rules, few-shot examples, tool definitions — and variable content (sources, question, timestamps) last. Use it for multi-turn chats, agents that resend long histories, and Q&amp;A over the same long document. With API providers, check the minimum cacheable length and how long caches live. Measure the hit rate in logs to confirm it works. Keep prompt templates stable across releases where possible.`,
        p: `Long instruction blocks are processed for every request, adding latency and cost that brings no new value. Caching the prefix removes that repeated work. A single timestamp at the top of the prompt can silently break caching, so prompt layout matters. For agents and long chats, where each turn resends the whole history, caching can cut both latency and input-token cost dramatically. The only requirement is disciplined prompt layout. It is one of the cheapest optimisations available.`,
        e: `A RAG prompt starts with a 900-token block of rules and one example, identical for every question. With vLLM <code>--enable-prefix-caching</code>, only the sources and the question (about 1,300 tokens) are processed per request, and time to first token drops noticeably. Moving a 'Today is <date>' line from the top of the prompt to the end restored the cache hit rate, which had silently dropped to zero. Time to first token fell back to its earlier level.` },
      { t: `Tensor & Pipeline Parallelism`,
        s: `Splitting one model across several GPUs: tensor parallelism splits each layer; pipeline parallelism splits groups of layers.`,
        h: `<b>Tensor parallelism</b> slices every weight matrix across GPUs; each GPU computes part of every layer and they exchange results after each layer, which needs fast interconnects (NVLink). <b>Pipeline parallelism</b> puts layers 1–20 on GPU 1 and 21–40 on GPU 2; micro-batches flow through like an assembly line. Large deployments combine both, plus expert parallelism for MoE models. Expert parallelism, used for MoE models, places different experts on different GPUs.`,
        u: `Needed when a model does not fit on one GPU, or to lower latency for very large models. In vLLM it is a flag (<code>--tensor-parallel-size</code>, <code>--pipeline-parallel-size</code>). Most small RAG deployments avoid it by choosing a model that fits one GPU (often quantised). Pipeline parallelism tolerates slower links between GPUs but adds latency; tensor parallelism lowers latency but needs fast interconnects. Benchmark both for your hardware.`,
        p: `A 70B model in 16-bit needs about 140 GB for weights alone — more than any single common GPU. Parallelism makes such models servable, at the cost of more complex infrastructure and inter-GPU communication. Parallelism also lowers latency for large models: splitting each layer's work across GPUs makes each token faster. But communication overhead grows with the number of GPUs, and failures of one GPU affect the whole model instance. Choosing a model that fits fewer GPUs is often simpler and cheaper overall.`,
        e: `A legal-tech company serves a 70B model for complex contract questions with tensor parallelism across two 80 GB GPUs, and a small 8B model on single GPUs for routine lookups. The router sends only hard questions to the 70B deployment. The small model handles about 90% of traffic, so the expensive multi-GPU deployment stays small. Quantising the 70B model to 4-bit later let it fit on fewer GPUs. Each change was re-evaluated before release.` },
      { t: `Time to First Token (TTFT)`,
        s: `How long the user waits until the first word of the answer appears.`,
        h: `TTFT = queueing time + prefill time (processing the whole prompt) + time for the first decode step. Prefill grows with prompt length, so long RAG prompts with many sources increase TTFT directly. Prefix caching, faster hardware, shorter contexts and lower queueing reduce it. After the first token, speed is governed by decode throughput. Network and gateway overhead add a little more, usually milliseconds.`,
        u: `TTFT is the latency users feel most in streaming chat. Track it at p50 and p95, set targets (e.g. under 1–2 s on GPU), and use it to size context budgets: every extra retrieved chunk adds prefill time. Also show progress stages ('Searching…', 'Reading sources…') so users know work is happening before the first token. Long TTFT on CPU makes such status messages essential. Report it separately from total answer time.`,
        p: `Users judge responsiveness by when text starts appearing. A long blank wait makes them think the system is broken, even if the total answer time is acceptable. Optimising TTFT improves perceived quality more than almost any other speed change. Every component before generation adds to TTFT: query rewriting, retrieval, reranking and validation steps that run first. Measuring TTFT per stage shows where to optimise. On slow hardware, prompt length dominates, so trimming context is the biggest lever.`,
        e: `On this CPU, prefill runs at about 36 tokens per second, so a 1,900-token prompt waits about 53 seconds before the first word. Capping sources at about 1,200 tokens cut TTFT to roughly 33 seconds; on a modern GPU the same prompt would start in well under a second. The chat page shows 'Picking the best passages…' and 'Writing the answer…' during that wait, which made the delay feel acceptable. The remaining wait is mostly hardware.` },
      { t: `Tokens per Second (Throughput)`,
        s: `How fast tokens are produced — for one request (decode speed) or across all requests (server throughput).`,
        h: `Per-request decode speed is limited mainly by memory bandwidth: each new token requires reading the active model weights (and KV cache). Server throughput adds batching: many requests share each weight read, so total tokens per second rises with concurrency until compute saturates. Prefill speed (prompt tokens per second) is separate and usually much higher on GPUs. Quantisation raises decode speed because fewer bytes are read per token.`,
        u: `Measure both prefill and decode speeds on your hardware for your model, then estimate answer times (TTFT + answer tokens ÷ decode speed) and capacity (users per server). Use the numbers to choose context sizes, max answer lengths and hardware. Benchmark with realistic prompts (your system prompt plus typical retrieved sources), because short test prompts overstate performance. Re-measure after changing quantisation, model or hardware.`,
        p: `Without measured speeds, teams set max tokens and context sizes by guesswork and end up with slow answers or overloaded servers. Measured throughput turns latency targets into concrete limits. Throughput also determines cost: tokens per second per GPU sets how many answers each machine produces per hour. Doubling throughput through batching or quantisation can halve serving cost. Measured numbers make capacity planning and budget discussions concrete.`,
        e: `Measured on this laptop with qwen2.5:3b: about 36 prompt tokens/s and about 7 output tokens/s, so a 300-token answer takes about 43 seconds to write after the prompt is read. That measurement drove the v2 design: 1,200 tokens of sources and 600 maximum answer tokens. Measuring took one request with the Ollama API, shown in the code below. Re-running it after each hardware or model change keeps the latency budget honest.`,
        c: `
import json, urllib.request
body = json.dumps({"model": "qwen2.5:3b", "prompt": prompt, "stream": False}).encode()
r = json.load(urllib.request.urlopen(urllib.request.Request(
        "http://127.0.0.1:11434/api/generate", body, {"Content-Type": "application/json"})))
print("prefill tok/s", r["prompt_eval_count"] / (r["prompt_eval_duration"] / 1e9))
print("decode tok/s", r["eval_count"] / (r["eval_duration"] / 1e9))` },
      { t: `Serving Engines (vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM, Ollama)`,
        s: `Software that runs LLMs efficiently behind an API.`,
        h: `<b>vLLM</b>: PagedAttention, continuous batching, prefix caching, many quantisation formats and an OpenAI-compatible API — the common choice for GPU serving. <b>TGI</b> (Hugging Face Text Generation Inference): production server with similar features. <b>TensorRT-LLM</b> (NVIDIA): compiled, maximum-performance kernels for NVIDIA GPUs. <b>Ollama</b>: very easy local serving of GGUF models on CPU, Apple Silicon or GPU, built on llama.cpp. SGLang is another fast option.`,
        u: `Use Ollama for laptops, prototypes and small single-user deployments; vLLM or TGI for multi-user GPU production; TensorRT-LLM when squeezing maximum performance from NVIDIA hardware. Put an LLM gateway (LiteLLM) in front so the application does not depend on one engine. Evaluate engines with your own model and prompt sizes: performance differs by hardware, model family and quantisation. Keep the engine version pinned like any other dependency.`,
        p: `A naive Python loop with Hugging Face transformers serves one request at a time slowly and wastes GPU memory. Serving engines provide batching, caching, quantisation and streaming APIs, giving many times more throughput on the same hardware. Engines also handle production details: health checks, metrics, streaming, stop sequences, LoRA adapters and model loading. Building these yourself takes months. Choosing the right engine early avoids rewriting the serving layer when traffic grows.`,
        e: `This platform runs Ollama on the CPU, called through LiteLLM. If it moved to a GPU server, the same LiteLLM call could point to a vLLM endpoint serving a larger model, without changing the application code — only the configured model name and URL. The evaluation suite would then confirm the larger model's quality before release, exactly as for any other pipeline change. That separation is the main reason to put a gateway in front of any engine.` },
    ]},
  ],
});
